{"name":"Cossack Dancer","hex":"#4d8aa1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cossack","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Cossack."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cossack"},{"term":"dancer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who dances, usually as a hobby, an occupation, or a profession.","A stripper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dancer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cossacks","blocks":[{"text":"The Cossacks are a predominantly East Slavic, Eastern Orthodox Christian militant people, originating from the Pontic–Caspian steppe of eastern Ukraine and southern Russia. Cossacks played an important role in defending the southern borders of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and of the Russian Empire, countering the Crimean–Nogai raids, alongside economically developing steppe regions north of the Black Sea and around the Azov Sea. Historically, they were a semi-nomadic and semi-militarized people, who were allowed a great degree of self-governance in exchange for military service under the nominal suzerainty of various Eastern European states. Although numerous ethnic, linguistic and religious groups came together to form the Cossacks, the East Slavs predominated, with other groups gradually coalesced and Slavicized, thereby adopting East Slavic culture, East Slavic languages and Eastern Orthodox Christianity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Ilja_Jefimowitsch_Repin_-_Reply_of_the_Zaporozhian_Cossacks_-_Yorck.jpg/330px-Ilja_Jefimowitsch_Repin_-_Reply_of_the_Zaporozhian_Cossacks_-_Yorck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cossacks","wikidata":"Q47805"},{"title":"Dance","blocks":[{"text":"Dance is an art form, consisting of sequences of body movements with aesthetic and often symbolic value, either improvised or purposefully selected. Dance can be categorized and described by its choreography, by its repertoire of movements or by its historical period or place of origin. Dance is typically performed with musical accompaniment, and sometimes with the dancer simultaneously using a musical instrument themselves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Two_dancers.jpg/330px-Two_dancers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dance","wikidata":"Q11639"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/AmericanCossacks.jpg/500px-AmericanCossacks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/AmericanCossacks.jpg/500px-AmericanCossacks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An American Cossack family in the 1950s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmericanCossacks.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Cossacks_in_parade_2015.jpg/500px-Cossacks_in_parade_2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Cossacks_in_parade_2015.jpg/500px-Cossacks_in_parade_2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cossacks marching in Red Square at the 2015 Victory Day Parade","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACossacks_in_parade_2015.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Fedir_Stovbynenko_-_Kozak-bandyryst_%281890%29.jpg/500px-Fedir_Stovbynenko_-_Kozak-bandyryst_%281890%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Fedir_Stovbynenko_-_Kozak-bandyryst_%281890%29.jpg/500px-Fedir_Stovbynenko_-_Kozak-bandyryst_%281890%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.o